From: timstarr@netcom.com (Tim Starr)
Date: Thu, 31 Mar 1994 21:34:29 -0800
Subject: [#94-4-3] Class Theory and Quigley

>From: "James H. Daugherty" <74204.3113@CompuServe.COM>
>
>...Frankly, people have to be taught to ignore for the most part the arguments
>... [of] the Court Intellectuals of the ruling class.

I agree.  Wasn't it the faculty of the University of Berlin that declared them-
selves the Intellectual Bodyguard of the House of Hohenzollern?

I would also reccomend exploring the libertarian class-theory of history, but
with the caveat that conspiracy theories can have their drawbacks as well as
their uses.  It's important to be able to argue a position on its own merits
before going into circumstantial ad hominems (which can be justified).

I've actually read one of the books on your list: Quigley's "Evolution of
Civilizations: An Introduction to Historical Analysis," so I've got a comment
or two on the brief review of it:

>Neo-Spenglerian theory of the rise and fall of civilization. 
>Claims civilization springs from a successful mechanism which allow an elite,
>ruling class to extract a "surplus" from the people for further expansion.
>Claims "institutionalization" eventually makes the mechanism rather than the
>extraction of surplus into an end in itself.  For instance Democracy itself
>becomes a value in itself rather than a fraud perpetrated by the elite to gain
>the consent of the ruled as originally intended! 

Quigley uses the term "social instrument of expansion" or "social instrument",
for short, to describe any means of accumulating surplus, that is, saving.  He
uses it in a politically neutral sense.  It doesn't have to refer to savings
acquired by means of coercion or by means of production, but it can refer to
either/both.  Institutionalization occurs when the social instrument is no
longer used to save up wealth to be invested in new technologies, but goes
directly into consumption.

I find his approach very useful as it applies to the present: information
technology is creating a new instrument of expansion which will surpass the
old, institutionalized corporate fascist welfare-warfare State, just as the
Industrial Revolution did to the mercantilist State.

