From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Date: Sat, 02 Apr 94 00:54:59 GMT
Subject: [#94-4-9] Simulation vs. Reality - part 2: sentient machines

Ray (rjc) replies:

>> If mechanistic theories of consciousness are true...
>
>   The negation meaning that consciousness is not subject to any natural law
>thus placing consciousness in the realm of the supernatural. More on
>this later.

My ideal theory would conform to natural law and give me what I want, e.g.
genuinely sapient, sentient beings with subjective qualia, the prospect of
true copying and upgrading of individuals, provision of more varieties of
free will compared to the "mechanistic" approach, and solving of NP-
complete problems in polynomial time (although that last condition I
regard as rather a luxury option).  I'll try to keep "ghosts" and fifth
forces out of it, although indeterminism is useful to me.  (Actually I've
decided to try and join the reductionists, and now maintain that my
approach is in a way more reductionist than that of the "mechanists"!)

The mechanists have got two choices [and I don't think many would go for
1) these days]: 

1)  They can adopt the philosophical "barefoot" behaviourism, popular until
about the fifties / sixties (particularly with the Soviets).  If men are
essentially just unconscious automata, whose output can be consistently
predicted from their sensory inputs, it's quite likely that simulation of
their particles on a machine would successfully simulate each individual.
After all, the simulation needn't be conscious.  However, this leads to a
form of solipsism -- it can't be applied to oneself (except for people who
claim to be zombies), and one's own mental states still exist in an
unexplained mental world.  (Whether or not this is technically a fallacy of
self-exclusion, it sure sounds like it!)

2)  They can take an epiphenomenal view.  Admit that men have a rich
variety of mental experiences including qualia such as color vision, but
these have no effects on behaviour.  (A variation on this is the
qualophobic approach, which claims that we're too stupid to have knowledge
of our own mental states, and if we think we believe something, we're
probably mistaken.  True, this may apply to very short durations only.)
Brains are just digital computers, and qualia are just a by-product of the
input ---> output computation.  Ergo copy the algorithms or simulate them
by simulating particles, and the first person subjective events will also
be reproduced.  

Now this is certainly possible, but it seems quite an extraordinary claim
compared to more modest proposals that consciousness requires machines
rather more structurally identical to a brain.  Admittedly, rather than
stick with the reductive materialist / identity theorist's claim that each
mental state must tie up with a specific physical state of the brain, it
seems reasonable to accept the functionalist view that the same mental
state could be reproduced using other types of hardware, e.g. silicon.
It's unlikely that the chemistry is important.  

The question then is, just how far can functionalism be taken?  If the
brain really is nothing but a pre-von Neuman computer and the sole function
of each neuron is to operate as a multi-input programmable logic gate,
then a universal Turing machine could simulate it.  I suggest that
consciousness and intelligence are two distinct properties.  Does anyone
think that the best chess-playing programs are even a tiny bit conscious
of what they're doing, as in having first person subjective experiences?
Do they feel even a tiny bit pissy when they're beaten?  It's clearly
possible to have a very clever algorithm that would never feel pain.  On
the other hand, just because an animal is less intelligent than some
programs, only able to discriminate about a dozen percepts or mental
states, this doesn't mean it lacks sentience and cannot feel its internal
states.  I'd divide into categories like this:

Sentience / consciousness                       Intelligence
-------------------------                       ------------

can be achieved with a brain-type               algorithms
structure -- a complex neural net               universal Turing machines
which operates by the generation and
transmission of electrical signals

Combine the two, and you get sapience.  Fortunately a brain can play both
roles, since acting as a computational device is one of its major
functions, at least.

Let's say we had a room designed to simulate a Chinese lady -- a human or
robot operator reads in questions in Chinese which are asked of it, the
operator writes numbers and symbols on a few blackboards, consults look up
tables, manipulates the numbers as necessary and eventually comes up with
a response.  Or maybe buttons are manipulated.  The room has no trouble
operating as a Turing machine, albeit very slowly, and may even pass a
Turing test (after allowances for speed).  The mechanists, though, make
the additional claim that a bunch of buttons or chalk particles can feel
pain, feel annoyed at times with the questioner, or at other times feel
pleased with herself (the chalk particles or buttons not the questioner!)
for having thought of a good reply.  (Stop erasing the blackboards and the
pain stops, for instance, as it would have to be the relationship between
several successive logical states.) 

Others have suggested that consciousness is an effect of differences in
heat between neurons as they fire.  An alternative hypothesis is that it's
related to the spiking frequency of electrochemical impulses in specific
neuronal structures.  I prefer taking an electromagnetic approach.  When
a neuron fires it's certainly acting as a logic gate, but this isn't
necessarily its only function.  It's also generating electromagnetic
waves.  Multiple functions from a single element, like when a single
stage in a reflex radio receiver amplifies both r.f. and a.f. signals,
could be expected to evolve due to their economy.  And, it turns out, this
model can lead to a better reduction of mental states to physical states.

Ironically, once you move too far from the identity theory of tying up
each possible mental state with a specific physical state, yet don't
revert to behaviourism, this leads towards a mild dualism.  You have to
assume that an individual's mental states mysteriously arise just through
having an accurate enough algorithm, and it's not supposed to matter
whether buttons or ball-cocks and cisterns are used to run the algorithm.
Each mental state does not tie up with a corresponding physical state,
only a logical state.  Therefore qualia cannot be reduced to physical
states, hence these physical states are independent of the "mind".

In my alternative, each possible mental state, however complex, could
be represented by its own particular landscape involving the fifth
dimension of electromagnetic space.  Let's say you're conceiving of a group
of people living in a muddy commune, one section stocked up with Molotov
cocktails for the revolution, another with booze and crack for their
recreation.  Each concept would have its particular meme or neuronal
structure; the total mental state would be described by all of these
firing, maybe with various spiking frequencies or amplitudes to define the
relative intensity of each element.  The full result would be a complex set
of e.m. waves relating to this mental state, perpendicular to the world-
line of the individual in the volume of space occupied by the brain.  With
electromagnetic waves explained as ripples in the fifth dimension, a given
mental state would be represented by a set of fifth-dimension warps along
a section of the individual's world-line approximately 200-400 mS in
length.  Since stronger signals with a longer range such as mains hum
would attenuate less sharply across the individual's world-line compared
to true signals emanating from neurons and would not correspond to any
learned memetic signal, they would be rejected as meaningless noise.

It may be argued that this leads to a sort of panpsychism (where even
electrons are conscious!), but particles have no means for representing
various concepts, learning concepts, or learning what alternative mental
states can denote.  Thus, they're just as clueless and unconscious as 
viruses and virions.  And if minds are just logical states, then they're
being formed all the time by the regular motions of planets and stars, or
if you happen to pick the right molecules in some fluid over a period.

>  This thought experment still doesn't refute my claim: a perfect simulation
>is as good as the real thing. If a ghost exists it is either 1)
>totally random so we can't in principle represent it finitely in some
>machine or 2) it is not random, and some encoding exists which can
>simulate it perfectly. If #1 is the situation, the ghost's behavior
>is as useful as flipping a coin.

If consciousness is solely a succession of logical states, then your
comments about ghosts are certainly correct, and the simulation could be
reality, particularly if multiple universe theories are true.  However, if
multiple universe theories are false, then dualism and/or mysticism are
necessary so it doesn't look like ghosts could be eliminated from the
simulation.

If consciousness requires _anything_ other than a succession of logical
states, and the universe is not a simulation, then consciousness could not
be simulated since a simulation of the particles would not reproduce the
necessary physical conditions.  This would even apply if consciousness has
no effects on behaviour; the simulation would behave properly but would be
unconscious or its subjective experiences would be inaccurate.

If the universe is a simulation then our consciousness is likely just a
succession of logical states.  But then it might be said that the simulated
beings were already simulations, therefore they hadn't successfully 
simulated themselves even if they thought they had -- it was an illusion.  

In part 3 I'll explain some advantages of the "mechanistic-plus" theory.

Steve Whitrow                           sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk

